$DIR/main.p:44:2: cannot use Named as T in Send: Named has field name of type string, which is not portable: a string's bytes live in the sender's address space
    A portable type can be sent between address spaces, such as two operating
    system processes that share no memory. Its values mean the same to the
    receiver as to the sender because they refer to nothing in the sender's
    memory: they are made only of numbers, booleans, enums, packed types, and
    arrays, structs and unions of those.
    Go has one address space, so any value can be passed anywhere and a string
    or pointer always means the same thing. A Plutonium kernel and its processes
    have separate address spaces, and the compiler checks what may cross instead
    of leaving the receiver to read a stale address.
    Instead of name, send the bytes in a fixed-size array, with their length as
    a number:
        name [16]byte
        nameLen uint8
    (see "Portable types" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2018" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:45:2: cannot use Linked as T in Send: Linked has field next of type *Linked, which is not portable: a pointer holds an address in the sender's address space
    A portable type can be sent between address spaces, such as two operating
    system processes that share no memory. Its values mean the same to the
    receiver as to the sender because they refer to nothing in the sender's
    memory: they are made only of numbers, booleans, enums, packed types, and
    arrays, structs and unions of those.
    Go has one address space, so any value can be passed anywhere and a string
    or pointer always means the same thing. A Plutonium kernel and its processes
    have separate address spaces, and the compiler checks what may cross instead
    of leaving the receiver to read a stale address.
    Instead of next, send the value it points to, or an index or offset as a
    number
    (see "Portable types" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2018" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:46:2: cannot use Reader as T in Send: Reader is not portable: an interface value refers to a type and to data in the sender's address space
    A portable type can be sent between address spaces, such as two operating
    system processes that share no memory. Its values mean the same to the
    receiver as to the sender because they refer to nothing in the sender's
    memory: they are made only of numbers, booleans, enums, packed types, and
    arrays, structs and unions of those.
    Go has one address space, so any value can be passed anywhere and a string
    or pointer always means the same thing. A Plutonium kernel and its processes
    have separate address spaces, and the compiler checks what may cross instead
    of leaving the receiver to read a stale address.
    Instead, send a union with one variant for each type the interface can hold:
        type Shape union {
            Circle struct{ r float64 }
            Rect   struct{ w, h float64 }
        }
    (see "Portable types" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2018" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:47:2: cannot use Frame as T in Send: Frame is not portable: an owner must be dropped exactly once, and sending it would also transfer that duty, which the structural rule does not express
    A portable type can be sent between address spaces, such as two operating
    system processes that share no memory. Its values mean the same to the
    receiver as to the sender because they refer to nothing in the sender's
    memory: they are made only of numbers, booleans, enums, packed types, and
    arrays, structs and unions of those.
    Go has one address space, so any value can be passed anywhere and a string
    or pointer always means the same thing. A Plutonium kernel and its processes
    have separate address spaces, and the compiler checks what may cross instead
    of leaving the receiver to read a stale address.
    Instead, send the plain data the owner is made of, in a struct that is not
    declared owner, and let the receiver build its own owner
    (see "Portable types" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2018" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:48:2: cannot use Outer as T in Send: Outer has field parts[].buf of type []byte, which is not portable: a slice refers to memory in the sender's address space
    A portable type can be sent between address spaces, such as two operating
    system processes that share no memory. Its values mean the same to the
    receiver as to the sender because they refer to nothing in the sender's
    memory: they are made only of numbers, booleans, enums, packed types, and
    arrays, structs and unions of those.
    Go has one address space, so any value can be passed anywhere and a string
    or pointer always means the same thing. A Plutonium kernel and its processes
    have separate address spaces, and the compiler checks what may cross instead
    of leaving the receiver to read a stale address.
    Instead of parts[].buf, send the elements in a fixed-size array, with their
